# The Authorization context had NO hecksagon at all -- so BindingPolicy's
# `default_binding` applied silently, and BOTH Policy and RoleAssignment
# were persisted_by("Memory") : wiped every single CLI/MCP dispatch, a
# fresh OS process each time. Confirmed live (2026-08-15, i774) :
# `Authorization::RoleAssignment.Active` returned `rows: []` and
# `state pr-agent` returned null immediately after a session that had
# supposedly just established 75 role assignments -- nothing survived the
# process boundary. The roster's own doc string ("Idempotent : the roster
# re-asserts each boot") only means anything if the roster is DURABLE
# across boots, which it never was.
#
# i773 misdiagnosed this as an upsert-by-natural-key DUPLICATION bug
# ("68 -> 75 records"). It isn't : a Heki-backed repro of the exact same
# identified_by-block + no-reference_to Assign shape proves the runtime
# correctly raises AlreadyExists on a repeat natural key (hydrate's
# `creates?` branch, hecksagain gem, `runtime/command_interpreter.rb` --
# deliberate, mutation-test-provenanced, not touched here). And that
# refusal is SAFE to re-assert against : `PolicyInterpreter#deliver`
# rescues every `DOMAIN_REFUSALS` member (AlreadyExists included) per
# policy, logs `delivered: false`, and does not fail the cascade. So
# binding these two aggregates to Heki does not risk a second `establish`
# exploding -- it only makes the FIRST successful assertion durable.
#
# See aggregates/framework/authorization/bluebook/authorization.world for
# the `dir :default` value half of this wiring.
Hecks.hecksagon "Authorization" do
  Authorization::Policy.persisted_by("Heki")
  Authorization::RoleAssignment.persisted_by("Heki")
end
